0;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:8pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;Resignations: &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:1.5pt 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:8pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Title&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:1.5pt 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:8pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:1.5pt 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:8pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Resigned&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="border-top:1pt solid #000;padding:1.5pt 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:8pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;President&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="border-top:1pt solid #000;padding:1.5pt 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:8pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;David Slater&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="border-top:1pt solid #000;padding:1.5pt 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:8pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;March 2026&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:1.5pt 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:8pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Executive Vice President and Chief Administrative Officer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:1.5pt 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:8pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Melissa Cox&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:1.5pt 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:8pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;May 2026&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</ferc:DisclosureOfChangesInOfficersDirectorsMajorSecurityHoldersAndVotingPowersOfTheRespondentTextBlock>
  <ferc:DisclosureOfSignificantEventThatRespondentParticipatesInACashManagementProgramsTextBlock id="f-23" contextRef="c-1">&lt;div style="margin-top:2pt;padding-right:18pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;13. Not applicable&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</ferc:DisclosureOfSignificantEventThatRespondentParticipatesInACashManagementProgramsTextBlock>
  <ferc:DisclosureOfImportantDisclosuresRegardingTheFinancialStatementsTextBlock id="f-24" contextRef="c-1">&lt;div style="margin-top:2pt;padding-right:18pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;Basis of Presentation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:6pt;padding-left:18pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;These financial statements have been prepared in accordance with the accounting requirements of the FERC. We have condensed or omitted certain information or notes pursuant to FERC rules and regulations. Therefore, these financial statements should be read in conjunction with our Annual Report on FERC Form No. 2 for the year ended December 31, 2025, which includes a summary of significant accounting policies and other disclosures. We have made normal and recurring adjustments that are necessary for a fair presentation of the financial results for the interim period presented.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:3pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;Long-Term Debt &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:6pt;margin-top:3pt;padding-left:18pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;In April 2026, Guardian entered into a credit agreement and borrowed $150 million under an unsecured term loan. The term loan matures on April 30, 2033 and bears interest at a variable rate equal to SOFR plus 1.95%. The proceeds were used to repay intercompany borrowings in the amount of $134 million.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:2pt;padding-right:18pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;Rate Case &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:2pt;padding-left:18pt;padding-right:18pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;In April 2022, in Docket No. RP22-725-001, the Federal Energy Regulatory Commission commenced an investigation of Guardian's rates pursuant to Section 5 of the Natural Gas Act. Guardian filed an agreed-upon Stipulation and Offer of Settlement with the FERC in October 2022, which was approved by the FERC in February 2023.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:2pt;padding-left:18pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;For our contracts under maximum tariff rates, the Settlement resulted in a decrease in revenue of approximately 5%. The impact of the Settlement remained consistent through March 31, 2025, with an additional decrease of 10% in the period April 1, 2025 through March 31, 2026, and a final additional decrease of 5% starting April 1, 2026.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</ferc:DisclosureOfImportantDisclosuresRegardingTheFinancialStatementsTextBlock>
  <ferc:DisclosureOfSubsequentEventsTextBlock id="f-25" contextRef="c-1">&lt;div style="margin-top:2pt;padding-right:18pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;Subsequent Events&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:3pt;padding-left:18pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Subsequent events are events or transactions that occur after the financial statement date but before the financial statements are issued or are available to be issued. The Company has evaluated subsequent events through August&#160;28, 2026, as this was the date financial statements were made available to be issued.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:18pt"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:18pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%"&gt;On July 31, 2026, in Docket No. RP26-1030-000, Guardian Pipeline filed with the FERC a Petition for Approval of Pre-Filing Stipulation and Settlement Agreement. The proposed settlement is intended to resolve certain rate-related matters without the need for a contested general rate case proceeding and provides for a two-phase implementation of new rates, with Phase I effective October 1, 2026 and Phase II effective July 1, 2027, or the first day of the month following the in-service date of the Bluff Creek Replacement project, whichever is later. The settlement includes a rate moratorium, under which no party may seek to make effective a change in the settlement rates prior to November 1, 2029, and a comeback provision that requires Guardian to file a Natural Gas Act ("NGA") section 4 general rate case that will cause new rates to take effect no later than November 1, 2031, unless Guardian has entered into a pre-filing settlement that is supported, not opposed, or not objected to by the settling parties identified in the settlement, or an NGA section 5 general rate case regarding Guardian's rates has been filed on or before November 1, 2031. The settlement agreement remains subject to FERC approval.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</ferc:DisclosureOfSubsequentEventsTextBlock>
  <ferc:GeneralDescriptionOfConstructionOverheadTextBlock id="f-26" contextRef="c-1">&lt;div style="margin-top:9pt;padding-right:18pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;1. Capitalization of Construction Overhead&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:9pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%"&gt;(a) Construction overhead from engineering, supervisory and administrative personnel includes the portion of salaries, benefits, and other expenses of general office and field employees specifically identified as supporting construction activities. (b) In lieu of direct charging of overhead to capital projects, a capital overhead burden rate is used to assign overhead dollars to capital projects. (c) Each month, direct charges on a capital project are multiplied by the overhead burden rate to calculate overhead charges applied to the project.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:9pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%"&gt;(b) Annually, a per department analysis of capital related obligations is performed, assessing the department&#8217;s functions and individual employee responsibilities. The calculated allocation rates are multiplied by each department&#8217;s budgeted expenses for the year and the resulting amounts are applied to a capital overhead pool for the year. The capital overhead pool is divided by the forecasted direct capital spend associated with an applicable construction period in which capital labor would be allocable for the year to determine the capital overhead burden rate. Direct capital spend for vehicles, IT equipment and etc. is excluded from the capital total. (f) These amounts are booked as additions to the construction work in process account for each construction project receiving the overhead.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:9pt;padding-right:18pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;2. Capitalization of Allowance for Funds Used During Construction&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:9pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;The allowance for funds used during construction (AFUDC) consists, in general, of the net borrowed funds used for construction purposes and a reasonable rate of return on other funds when so used. The allowance is determined by applying the capitalization rate to construction work orders. Capitalization begins at the time the company begins the continuous accumulation of costs in a construction work order on a planned progressive basis and ends when the facilities are placed in the service for which constructed.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:9pt;padding-right:18pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;3. N/A&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</ferc:GeneralDescriptionOfConstructionOverheadTextBlock>
